if you've been itching for a vacation  you're not alone  summer holiday travel has surged to  levels not seen since before the covet  19 pandemic  the consumer price index which is the  benchmark measure of inflation  rose 0.9 percent in june the largest  one-month increase  since 2008 vacation-related expenses  weren't spared  prices increased for everything from new  cameras and swimsuits to rental cars and  hotel rooms  were just have this kind of collective  need to get out and about  experts say price increases reflect an  economy on the upswing from pandemic  lows  but higher demand has made some vacation  essentials harder to come by  here's why that is in some strategies to  help avoid the sticker shock  as lockdowns have eased summer holiday  travel has surged both  in the air and on the ground triple a  says more than 47 million people  traveled over the fourth of july holiday  weekend  which is 40 percent more than last year  but u.s air travel is still recovering  after the pandemic drove it to  a virtual standstill much of the  recovery lag has to do with the slow  return of business travel  however airlines prepared for an almost  normal schedule of flights this summer  as leisure travel demand returned  contributing to airfare increasing  nearly 25  since last year if you're looking for  cheaper airfare  wsj travel editor debra dunn suggests  switching up  when you fly the key to finding a less  expensive airfare is to be really  flexible  not just traveling on a weekend  or traveling from a saturday to sunday  but i would look at the price difference  if you fly on a tuesday or wednesday  but once you're all booked and touched  down at your destination  you still may hit those higher prices  vehicle rental prices are up  over 87 from a year ago the simple  reason is that rental companies  have less cars smaller rental fleets had  one big issue  summer demand surged over 300 in some  cities  according to kayak this has led some  travelers being turned away at the  rental counter because there aren't  enough cars on the lot  and with a semi-conductor shortage  continuing to hamper new car production  some analysts think it will be well into  2022 before fleet supplies are enough to  meet demand  book months and months ahead and i  wouldn't  book a non-refundable uh  reservation the other possibility is to  look at picking up a car in town rather  than in the airport  you'll have to take an uber from the  airport but it's still day to day  probably a better deal those with their  own set of wheels could count themselves  lucky if they weren't  facing the steepest gasoline prices in  nearly seven years  as gas shortages have plagued many  regions the colonial pipeline cyber  attack in may led to east coast  shortages right before the summer season  and some regions of the country have had  a hard time restocking gas stations  because of a lack of truck drivers  many of whom had been laid off during  the pandemic according to analysts  in the high prices aren't restricted to  travel expenses  men's and women's swimwear prices were  up 1.4 and 5.5 percent  respectively which analysts attribute to  pent up vacation demand  demand outpacing supply has hit other  vacation essentials as well  like cameras which have risen in price  by almost six percent  and depending on where your head hits  the pillow you could be paying more for  accommodations  room rates have recovered to  pre-pandemic levels  but the slow return of business travel  has kept occupancy rates slightly lower  than before the pandemic  according to analysts and while demand  may be high in traditional destinations  like florida and hawaii  experts say price increases are being  driven by vacationers booking bigger  accommodations that may be off the  beaten path  travelers are opting for larger hotel  suites or entire rental houses to  accommodate families vacationing  together  and according to air dna which tracks  short-term rental prices of airbnb  and verbo demand in small cities in  rural locations has surged over 51  since 2019. the current vacation season  is being defined by an economy in  recovery  and a population desperate for some r r  even if it puts a dent in their wallets  be flexible about where you're going to  go when you're going to go  how long you're going to stay  flexibility i think is the operative  word this summer  the last thing you want to be is  stressed out on vacation but if prices  continue to rise  a stress-free vacation may be harder to  come by
 

